Short superpermutations

The superpermutations in words/ are built with the construction of Jay Pantone,
https://github.com/jaypantone/superperm-upper-43-80 (Apache License 2.0). 2-loops, slices, the selection, the
completion, the transport and the closed trails are his.

The earlier words on 11, 12 and 13 symbols re-join the component words of his superpermutations (43,930,680,
522,745,581 and 6,747,918,066 letters): they only change where each component is opened and in what order the
components are joined. The later words on 10, 11, 12 and 13 symbols use other selections inside the same
construction. These selections are derived from his file tools/construction-input.txt; they differ from his on the
loops that his selection leaves without a slice, and some of them use slices with other numbers of visible classes,
read from his rules.

The gap-2 opening used for the n=11 and n=12 words was identified in rumstd's n=10 superpermutation of length 4,034,873
(pull request #3 to jaypantone/superperm-upper-43-80). The 43,930,644 word was found starting from rumstd's
43,930,668 word (pull request #4), which builds on Theo H.'s 43,930,678 word (pull request #2). The structure of the
n=10 word here (groups of seven small closed trails written as paths or closed into loops, in chains of six) was
read off the same word of rumstd, and the order of its groups is his.

Cutting a trail inside a 1-cycle, as tools/recut_wide.c and tools/word2plan.c do, was identified in Theo H.'s n=11
superpermutation of length 43,930,624 (pull request #6 to jaypantone/superperm-upper-43-80). The fixed-order pass of
tools/recut.c was found independently by Theo H. and by the authors of this repository. No code from Theo H.'s
repository (GPL-3.0-or-later) is included here; the programs in tools/, reproduce/, selection/, arrange/, recipes/,
ties/, proofs/ and words/n8/tools/ were written for this repository.

The newest words on 12 and 13 symbols in words/ (with their plans in plan/) start from William Echols's word of
522,737,174 letters (https://github.com/WilliamEchols/superpermutations, commit 3ba3bca) for n = 12 and from my
word of 6,747,802,393 letters for n = 13. They use two kinds of move that others found: re-opening a block of
closed trails (Echols) and moving one closed trail and choosing the cut at which it is opened (Theo H.,
https://github.com/Tholsh/superpermutation_study, commit 2d1965f). I wrote the moves again; no code of either
repository is used. The construction is Pantone's.

recipes/n14 publishes the word on 14 symbols as a generator and a plan, the form in which William Echols published
his words on 14 symbols. gen14.c can also write the order of his 2-lift (recipe "superperm-2lift/1", mode echols);
it is written from my own definitions, and no code of his repository is used.

Theo H.'s word of 46,173 letters on 8 symbols (https://github.com/Tholsh/superpermutation_study, data/n8/46173) was
found with tools/trailsearch.c of this repository on the selection of words/n8. It is his result; it is not
included here.

proofs/lean/constant61937, proofs/lean/constant305989 and proofs/lean/constant43586371 are more selections inside
Pantone's construction, proved in Lean on top of his library like proofs/lean/constant: the cycle re-choice
(constant61937), the block step (constant305989) and the block step applied again (constant43586371) are mine, the
construction is Pantone's. They use the patched copy of his
Assembly/SupportedStates.lean in proofs/lean/constant/patches and contain no file of his. proofs/lean/n8struct
imports the same library for rows, slices and closed trails and contains no file of his.

proofs/lean/constant/patches/SuperpermutationUpperBound/Assembly/SupportedStates.lean is a modified copy of the file
SuperpermutationUpperBound/Assembly/SupportedStates.lean of Jay Pantone's repository
https://github.com/jaypantone/superperm-upper-43-80 (commit c8fb7ffdd69be1375580710ae11b47a295b1f0f6, Apache License
2.0). Two changes: one import is replaced by another, and the section Partition.SprintFamily at the end of the file is
removed. The file says so at its top, and proofs/lean/constant/patches/SupportedStates.diff shows the change. The other
files of proofs/lean/constant were written for this repository on top of his library; four passages follow his text
and are named in proofs/lean/constant/README.md.

The lower bounds of proofs/lean/lower, proofs/lean/n7 and proofs/lean/n8_46134 are proved on top of Xiaolong Liu's
preimage-chain library (https://github.com/Haruhiyuki/superpermutations-preimage-chain-lower-bounds) and of the
Hunter-Raudvere library (https://github.com/urdvr/superpermutations-hunter); proofs/lean/asym uses the lower bound
of the Hunter-Raudvere library. Both libraries are used unchanged and are not included here.

proofs/lean/n8 holds changes to William Echols's superperm8-ge-46130
(https://github.com/williamechols/superperm8-ge-46130, commit 893ab2d92669ea56d012ceca626bf01ca631e920, Apache License
2.0): for three variants a diff against his files (variants/*/changes.diff, which contains lines of his files), and new
files. The files that proofs/lean/n8/build.sh puts together from a checkout of his repository are modified copies of
his work, and each changed file says at its top that it was changed. His NOTICE, which names the projects his is
derived from (superperm7-ge-5898, Copyright 2026 Anthropic, PBC, Apache License 2.0; superperm6, Copyright (c) 2026
Benjamin Grayzel, MIT License), is reproduced in proofs/lean/n8/NOTICE.echols.

words/n8/tools/ writes the completion slices, closed trails and port rule of Pantone's construction for n = 8,
with rows of every length read from his rules as in reproduce/REPRODUCE.md. It can read William Echols's n = 8
word of 46,178 letters (https://github.com/WilliamEchols/superpermutations) when a user supplies it; the word is
not included here.

ties/ lists words of other people (Teodorescu, Pantone, rumstd, Theo H.) by their SHA-256 only; the 85 words of
5,905 letters stored there are new and were made from Teodorescu's words by replacing the first or the last chain
of their kernel.

